<title>RUSSIA: Russian 1996 debt servicing steady at $7.1 bln.</title>
<headline>Russian 1996 debt servicing steady at $7.1 bln.</headline>
<dateline>MOSCOW 1997-06-10</dateline>
<text>
<p>Russia spent $7.1 billion on servicing its foreign debt in 1996, the same as in 1995, the Central Bank said in its annual balance of payments data.</p>
<p>The actual figure due in debt servicing fell to $18.1 billion in 1996 from $20.2 billion in 1995.</p>
<p>The bank issued the following figures:</p>
<p>    billions of dollars		   1996     1995</p>
<p>    payments due			    18.1     20.2</p>
<p>    actual payments			  7.1	7.1</p>
<p>debt service ratio (exports) pct:</p>
<p>    payments due			    18.3     22.1</p>
<p>    actual payments			  7.2	7.8</p>
<p>The Russian European Centre for Economic Policy issued the following figures for debt (billions of dollars), quoting the Ministry of Finance (January 1 of respective year).</p>
<p>						  1997     1996</p>
<p>    Total debt			     127.4    120.4</p>
<p>	Former Soviet Union		102.3    103.0</p>
<p>	  Official creditors		62.2     62.7</p>
<p>	  Commercial banks, firms	 40.1     40.4</p>
<p>	Russia (from 1992)		  25.1     17.4</p>
<p>	  Official creditors		 9.0	6.0</p>
<p>	  Commercial banks, firms	  1.3	0.0</p>
<p>	  IFIs				  15.8     11.4</p>
<p>NOTE - IFIs are international financial institutions.</p>
<p>--Jonathan Lynn, Moscow Newsroom, +7095 941 8520</p>
</text>
